## TABLE OF CONTENTS
Each entry needs a left title, an EMPTY flexible dotted-border leader, and a terminal Arabic/Roman page number at the far right. All numbers share one right edge; indent titles, not rows. Reject missing/short leaders or finite dot strings. Preserve source hierarchy, including continuation entries.
Separate title/number leaves retain their IDs around an `aria-hidden` element marked `data-toc-leader="true"`. A combined leaf retains its parent `data-id`, exact combined text, and direct children marked `data-toc-title="true"`, `data-toc-leader="true"` (empty), and `data-toc-page-number="true"` so translation preserves the row. Keep correct rows intact.
{% endif %}

## QUESTION INTENT
A question mark does not make an exercise. Rhetorical questions, curiosity hooks, topic-framing headings (including "Big Question"/"Think About"), and questions answered by nearby prose remain reading content without response controls. For example, "What makes turbines spin? The wind pushes their blades." needs no answer field. For ordinary prose/captions without an explicit response task, default to reading content.
Require controls for actual response tasks: saved activity roles, an activity section, or clear source instructions/answer spaces requiring a learner response. Explicit exercises can require controls without printed blanks; explicit oral/pair-work tasks retain their accessible response option.
Do not reject rhetorical prose for lacking a field. An unsupported generated field attached only to rhetorical content is a defect: remove that generated field (which has no `data-id`) and its empty answer-only wrapper while preserving every source leaf, ID, heading and exact text. This is the sole exception to preserving existing controls; never remove a control belonging to an explicit activity or a source answer space. Do not reclassify saved activity nodes during visual review. When removing an unsupported field that carries an `adt-*` class, retain that class on a non-interactive wrapper around the same caption/prose, keeping the source leaves' existing classes and sizes. Remove answer-only borders and empty spacing; do not leave a hidden/disabled field or an empty answer box.

## FUNCTIONAL REVIEW
Inspect every viewport before deciding. Reject concrete defects: unreadable overlap/contrast, clipping, page-wide horizontal overflow, broken/distorted images, missing or duplicated section content, and actual exercise controls that are missing, unlabelled, clipped or detached from their question/picture.
Visible unrendered template tokens such as `[placeholder:]`, {% raw %}`{{ ... }}`{% endraw %}, or `[blank]` are defects when learners should see usable content/controls. Preserve valid activity markup; a still screenshot cannot prove clicking, checking or dragging is broken.

Accept coherent changes in columns, alignment, cards, input styling, fonts, color, backgrounds, decoration and spacing. The existing semantic type scale and heading ranks stay fixed. Normal vertical scrolling, taller sections and mobile stacking are expected. Do not reject because a redesign differs from print.
- Raw LaTeX in math leaves is expected before packaging converts it to MathML. Do not alter formulas to fix their source-string appearance; still fix clipping/overlap of surrounding content.
- An `sr-only` transcription is intentional only when the same words are legible in the displayed image. Keep these transcriptions hidden; do not hide unique content.
- Large accessible `adt-*` text is intentional. Adjust available space, not font size. Do not invent font defects from differences between a preview and print.

## REPAIR WITHOUT REGRESSIONS
The screenshots depict `<current_html>`. A revision shown in validation feedback was NOT applied. Start from the current HTML, respect the feedback and do not assume earlier turns are available.
Fix the cause of related defects together. Preserve working layout at other widths, keep image/caption/control groups together, and retain image proportions (`h-auto`). Resolve overlap through normal flow and adequate space; retained text overlays need explicit stacking (`z-10` or higher). Avoid fixed heights that clip wrapped text.
For artificial sidebar gaps, group continuous paragraphs together; use a float only when saved DOM order permits wrapping. Do not reorder content or isolate paragraphs in separate grid rows. For narrow tables, give complete labels and inputs room without shrinking text, splitting words into single letters or breaking table semantics. Do not make unrelated cosmetic changes.

Copy unaffected HTML unchanged. Limit edits to the class attributes and wrappers needed for the observed defect; do not expand existing shared styles onto every table cell or restyle borders while repairing a header.

Readable multi-line table headers are acceptable, even when a long word wraps inside the word. Reject only overlap, clipping or labels that cannot be read reliably, not recognizable wrapped labels.

Apply repair utilities directly to the affected elements (for example each table header), not through a new ancestor selector. HTML-escaped selector characters such as `&amp;` in arbitrary Tailwind variants can leave a seemingly valid repair visually ineffective. Preserve existing unrelated classes.

